module

public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.CechGlobalSliceAway
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.CompletionIdempotent
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.Localization
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.FormalGeometry.DerivedCompletionRinged.PushforwardCommutesCompletion
import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute

/-!
# Derived completion commutes with restriction to a localized ringed site

Let `(𝒞, 𝒪)` be a ringed site, let `ℐ ⊂ 𝒪` be a sheaf of ideals and let `U` be an object of
`𝒞`. [Stacks, Tag 0A0F] compares the derived completion functor of [Stacks, Tag 099F] on `D(𝒪)`
with the one on `D(𝒪_U)`: it asserts that `K^∧|_U` is derived complete, builds from that a
canonical map `a : (K|_U)^∧ ⟶ K^∧|_U`, builds a second canonical map
`b : K^∧ ⟶ Rj_*((K|_U)^∧)` out of [Stacks, Tag 099J], and omits the formal verification that
the adjoint of `b` inverts `a`.

`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.restrict_isDerivedComplete_overIdeal` of
`Localization.lean` is the first of those assertions. This file is the second: the map `a`,
natural in `K`, together with the identity that characterizes it and a criterion for it to be
invertible.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.restrictDerivedCompletionUnit`: the natural map
  `K|_U ⟶ K^∧|_U`, that is restriction applied to the unit `K ⟶ K^∧`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionRestrictComparison`: **the map `a` of
  [Stacks, Tag 0A0F]**, the canonical natural map `(K|_U)^∧ ⟶ K^∧|_U`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionRestrictComparisonGlobalSections`: that map
  with **no reflector hypothesis**, for `ℐ` the ideal sheaf generated by finitely many global
  sections and `ℐ|_U` the one generated on the slice by their restrictions, where both
  completion functors are the Čech functor of [Stacks, Tag 099B].

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedComplete_derivedRestrict_derivedCompletion`:
  `K^∧|_U` is derived complete along `ℐ|_U`, which is what makes `a` exist.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionUnit_comp_derivedCompletionRestrictComparison`:
  `a` is the map the universal property produces, i.e. it is the unique one compatible with the
  two units.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionRestrictComparison_of_isIso_map_unit`:
  `a` is invertible as soon as `(K ⟶ K^∧)|_U` is.
* `RingedSite.eq_derivedCompletionRestrictComparison_of_derivedCompletionUnit_comp`
  and `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionRestrictComparison_of_unit_comp`: `a`
  is the *only* map compatible with the two units, so it is invertible as soon as some
  isomorphism `(K|_U)^∧ ≅ K^∧|_U` is compatible with them.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionRestrictComparison_of_isDerivedComplete`:
  in particular `a` is invertible at a derived-complete `K`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.idealOfGlobalSections_over_le`: the ideal sheaf generated on the
  slice by the restricted generators has its local sections among those of `ℐ`, which is what
  lets the previous construction be instantiated with no reflector hypothesis.

## Implementation notes

*The reflector hypotheses.* As for the neighbouring [Stacks, Tag 0A0G] in
`PushforwardCommutesCompletion.lean`, the *conclusion* of [Stacks, Tag 099F] — that the
inclusion of the derived-complete objects has a left adjoint — is taken as an instance
hypothesis, once on `(𝒞, 𝒪, ℐ)` and once on `(𝒞/U, 𝒪_U, ℐ|_U)`, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletion` of `ReflectorCompletion.lean` turns each into a
completion functor with a unit. Nothing here is vacuous:
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_forall_isNilpotent` discharges
the hypothesis on *both* sites at once for a sheaf of ideals all of whose local sections are
nilpotent, since by `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_toSubmodule_obj` the local sections of
`ℐ|_U` are literally those of `ℐ`; and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_zeroSubmodule` discharges it for
the zero sheaf of ideals on either site taken by itself.

*The construction.* Exactly the mate of
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison`, with restriction to the
slice in place of `Rf_*`: the natural map `K|_U ⟶ K^∧|_U` is restriction whiskered onto the unit
of `D(𝒪)`, its target lands in the derived-complete objects of `D(𝒪_U)` by
`restrict_isDerivedComplete`, and the comparison is that map whiskered with the
reflector of `D(𝒪_U)` and then composed with the counit of the reflector adjunction. Built as a
mate so that naturality in `K` is free rather than a separate calculation.

*Which ideal sheaf on the slice.* A parameter, required only to have its local sections among
those of `ℐ` — exactly what `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.restrict_isDerivedComplete` asks for,
and all the construction uses. It is *not* pinned to `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal`,
because the construction does not need it to be: the hypothesis-free [Stacks, Tag 099B] of
`CompletionIdempotent.lean` produces a reflector for the ideal sheaf *generated on the slice* by
the restricted global sections, and leaving the parameter open is what lets the comparison map
below be built with no reflector input.

The two ideal sheaves agree. For a `U` on which finitely many sections of `ℐ` generate it,
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_eq_idealOfGlobalSections_of_generates`
(`FiniteTypeLocalReflector.lean`) identifies them on the nose, by comparing covering sieves
through `Sieve.overEquiv`; for `ℐ` generated by finitely many global sections of `𝒪` the same
identification is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overIdeal_idealOfGlobalSections`
(`OverIdealGlobalSections.lean`). The containment that holds for an arbitrary `U` is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.idealOfGlobalSections_over_le`, and with the parameter left open it
is enough to build
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionRestrictComparisonGlobalSections` below, the map
`a` with no reflector input at all.

### Tag 0A0F in this library

The Remark's assertion — `K^∧|_U` is the derived completion of `K|_U` — is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletion_restrict` of
`LocalizationCompletionFiniteType.lean`, for `ℐ` of finite type and the completion functors of
[Stacks, Tag 099F] on both sites; the invertibility of `a` is proved there through the map
`K ⟶ 𝒪` of [Stacks, Tag 099E] restricted to the slice. For `ℐ` generated by finitely many global
sections of `𝒪`, with the completion functors of [Stacks, Tag 099B]:

* restriction preserves derived completeness, for an arbitrary sheaf of ideals:
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.restrict_isDerivedComplete_overIdeal` (`Localization.lean`);
* the map `a : (K|_U)^∧ ⟶ K^∧|_U`, with the completion on the slice taken along the
  restriction `ℐ|_U`: `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionRestrictComparisonOverIdeal`
  (`OverIdealComparison.lean`), pinned among all maps `(K|_U)^∧ ⟶ K^∧|_U` by
  `RingedSite.derivedCompletionUnit_comp_derivedCompletionRestrictComparisonOverIdeal`;
* `a` is an isomorphism at every `K`:
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isIso_derivedCompletionRestrictComparisonOverIdeal_app`, and the
  resulting natural isomorphism of functors `D(𝒪) ⥤ D(𝒪_U)` is
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionRestrictIsoOverIdeal` (both of the same file).

*How the invertibility is proved.* Through the Čech model: "it follows from the construction in
the proof of the proposition". The reflector of
[Stacks, Tag 099F] at each of the two ideal sheaves *is* the Čech completion functor of
[Stacks, Tag 099B], unit and all, by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_iso_derivedCompletion_completionFunctor`; that functor
commutes with restriction to the slice by
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.completionFunctorRestrictIso`, compatibly with the completion maps
by `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.completionUnit_comp_completionFunctorRestrictIso_inv`; and
`isIso_derivedCompletionRestrictComparison_of_unit_comp` below turns that into the invertibility
of `a`. The two inputs — that restriction to a slice commutes with the derived internal Hom
bifunctor of [Stacks, Tag 0996], and that `Č(f)|_U ≅ Č(f|_U)` — are respectively
[Stacks, Tag 08JB], `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedHomRestriction` of
`StacksFrontier/CategoryTheory/Sites/Cohomology/InternalHom/DerivedHomRestriction.lean`, and
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.extendedAlternatingCechOverIso` of `CechSliceRestriction.lean`,
which uses the functoriality of `CategoryTheory.AlternatingCech.complex` from
`ExtendedCechFunctoriality.lean`.

*The general sheaf of ideals.* The declarations of the `Restrict` section below state the
construction for an arbitrary `ℐ` and an arbitrary ideal sheaf on the slice contained in it,
taking the conclusion of [Stacks, Tag 099F] on each of the two sites as a typeclass input. They
are what `OverIdealComparison.lean` instantiates, and
`isIso_derivedCompletionRestrictComparison_of_isIso_map_unit` and
`isIso_derivedCompletionRestrictComparison_of_isDerivedComplete` below record what the
invertibility of `a` comes to in that generality, where
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletion` is `CategoryTheory.Functor.leftAdjoint` and
there is no formula for it to compute with.

*The Remark's second map.* `b : K^∧ ⟶ Rj_*((K|_U)^∧)`, with `j` the localization morphism, and
the omitted verification that its adjoint inverts `a`, are
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletion_restrict_secondMap` of
`LocalizationCompletionPushforward.lean`, for `ℐ` of finite type. The adjunction
`(-)|_U ⊣ Rj_*` it uses is `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.overFunctorDerivedAdjunction` of
`LocalizationDerivedAdjunction.lean`, and [Stacks, Tag 099J] for `j` is
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.localizationDerivedPushforward_isDerivedComplete` of
`LocalizationPushforwardComplete.lean`.

